@page "/"
@inject ChallengeConfig Config

<PageTitle>Santa's Workshop — 12 Days of MongoDBmas (C#)</PageTitle>

<section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">12 Days of MongoDBmas · C# edition</p>
        <h1>Santa's workshop needs a developer</h1>

        @* The scene. Kept in step with Part 1's story (the ledger, the elves, the cocoa) so a
           reader who clicks through meets the same workshop, not a different one. *@
        <p class="hero__lede">
            Santa's entire toy catalogue lives in one enormous leather ledger, written in
            fountain pen. It is four thousand pages long. Three elves have repetitive strain
            injury. And last Tuesday, somebody spilled cocoa on the entire letter M.
        </p>

        @* The promise: what they will actually do, in the order they will do it. *@
        <p>
            You've been brought in to rescue it. Across five hands-on C# challenges you'll move
            the catalogue into <strong>MongoDB</strong>, settle who's been naughty or nice, and
            help the elves find the perfect gift for any child — even one who can't spell
            “dinosaur”. Then you'll build an AI assistant that has read the whole Elf Handbook,
            so the elves don't have to.
        </p>

        @* The logistics, last: it's free, it's low-pressure, and there is a reward. *@
        <p>
            Each part ends with a <strong>free MongoDB skill badge</strong>. No experience needed —
            go at your own pace, because nothing expires.
        </p>

        <div class="actions">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="setup">Get set up</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="parts">See the challenges</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="wrap">
    <h2>How it works</h2>

    <ol class="steps">
        <li>
            <h3>Get your environment running</h3>
            <p>
                Open <a href="@Config.Repo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the repo</a> in GitHub
                Codespaces and everything is waiting for you.
                Prefer to work locally? Docker or a free Atlas cluster both work.
                <a href="setup">The setup page</a> walks through all three.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>Read the part's README</h3>
            <p>
                It tells the story, then walks you through numbered steps. Each step
                teaches one thing and maps to one method you're about to write.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>Fill in the <code>// TODO</code>s</h3>
            <p>
                Every part has a <code>Starter/</code> project with the method signatures
                already there and the bodies left to you.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>Run the tests until they're green</h3>
            <p>
                They start red. When they pass, you've got it — and you're ready for the
                badge. Stuck? Every part has a <code>SOLUTIONS.md</code>, and using it
                isn't cheating.
            </p>
            <pre><code>dotnet test part1-inventory/Tests</code></pre>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What you'll build</h2>

    <ul class="cards">
        @foreach (var part in Config.Parts)
        {
            <li>
                <div class="card">
                    <p class="card__step">Part @part.Id · @part.Level</p>
                    <h3>@part.Title</h3>
                    <p>@part.Summary</p>
                    @* A release date only for parts that are not out yet. A released part needs no
                       badge saying so — and a green pill looks like a button. *@
                    @if (!part.Available)
                    {
                        <p><span class="tag tag--date">Releases @part.Released</span></p>
                    }
                </div>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:1.5rem"><a href="parts">Full details for every part →</a></p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>About the badges.</strong> Each part lines up with a free MongoDB
            skill badge. The badges assess MongoDB <em>concepts</em> — they aren't a C#
            exam, and they don't look at your code. You don't have to do the exercises
            to take one, but they teach exactly what the assessments cover.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every part's README ends with a checklist mapping each badge objective to
            the step that teaches it, plus a <em>"From C# to the badge"</em> table
            translating the C# you just wrote into the shell and Atlas UI syntax the
            assessment uses.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
